DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ccppuusseett((99)) family of macros provide a flexible and efficient CPU set
     implementation, backed by the bitset(9) macros.  Each CPU is represented
     by a single bit.  The maximum number of CPUs representable by _c_p_u_s_e_t___t is
     _C_P_U___S_E_T_S_I_Z_E.  Individual CPUs in cpusets are referenced with indices zero
     through _C_P_U___S_E_T_S_I_Z_E _- _1.

     The CCPPUUSSEETT__TT__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR() macro allows one to initialize a _c_p_u_s_e_t___t with
     a compile time literal value.

     The CCPPUUSSEETT__FFSSEETT() macro defines a compile time literal, usable by
     CCPPUUSSEETT__TT__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR(), representing a full cpuset (all CPUs present).
     For examples of CCPPUUSSEETT__TT__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR() and CCPPUUSSEETT__FFSSEETT() usage, see the
     _C_P_U_S_E_T___T___I_N_I_T_I_A_L_I_Z_E_R _E_X_A_M_P_L_E section.

     The CCPPUU__CCLLRR() macro removes CPU _c_p_u___i_d_x from the cpuset pointed to by
     _c_p_u_s_e_t.  The CCPPUU__CCLLRR__AATTOOMMIICC() macro is identical, but the bit represent‐
     ing the CPU is cleared with atomic machine instructions.

     The CCPPUU__CCOOPPYY() macro copies the contents of the cpuset _f_r_o_m to the cpuset
     _t_o.  CCPPUU__CCOOPPYY__SSTTOORREE__RREELL() is similar, but copies component machine words
     from _f_r_o_m and writes them to _t_o with atomic store with release semantics.
     (That is, if _t_o is composed of multiple machine words,
     CCPPUU__CCOOPPYY__SSTTOORREE__RREELL() performs multiple individually atomic operations.)

     The CCPPUU__SSEETT() macro adds CPU _c_p_u___i_d_x to the cpuset pointed to by _c_p_u_s_e_t,
     if it is not already present.  The CCPPUU__SSEETT__AATTOOMMIICC() macro is identical,
     but the bit representing the CPU is set with atomic machine instructions.
     The CCPPUU__SSEETT__AATTOOMMIICC__AACCQQ() macro sets the bit representing the CPU with
     atomic acquire semantics.

     The CCPPUU__IISSSSEETT() macro returns true if CPU _c_p_u___i_d_x is a member of the
     cpuset pointed to by _c_p_u_s_e_t.

     The CCPPUU__ZZEERROO() macro removes all CPUs from _c_p_u_s_e_t.

     The CCPPUU__FFIILLLL() macro adds all CPUs to _c_p_u_s_e_t.

     The CCPPUU__SSEETTOOFF() macro removes all CPUs in _c_p_u_s_e_t before adding only CPU
     _c_p_u___i_d_x.

     The CCPPUU__EEMMPPTTYY() macro returns true if _c_p_u_s_e_t is empty.

     The CCPPUU__IISSFFUULLLLSSEETT() macro returns true if _c_p_u_s_e_t is full (the set of all
     CPUs).

     The CCPPUU__FFFFSS() macro returns the 1-index of the first (lowest) CPU in
     _c_p_u_s_e_t, or zero if _c_p_u_s_e_t is empty.  Like with ffs(3), to use the non-
     zero result of CCPPUU__FFFFSS() as a _c_p_u___i_d_x index parameter to any other
     ccppuusseett((99)) macro, you must subtract one from the result.

     The CCPPUU__CCOOUUNNTT() macro returns the total number of CPUs in _c_p_u_s_e_t.

     The CCPPUU__SSUUBBSSEETT() macro returns true if _n_e_e_d_l_e is a subset of _h_a_y_s_t_a_c_k.

     The CCPPUU__OOVVEERRLLAAPP() macro returns true if _c_p_u_s_e_t_1 and _c_p_u_s_e_t_2 have any com‐
     mon CPUs.  (That is, if _c_p_u_s_e_t_1 AND _c_p_u_s_e_t_2 is not the empty set.)

     The CCPPUU__CCMMPP() macro returns true if _c_p_u_s_e_t_1 is NOT equal to _c_p_u_s_e_t_2.

     The CCPPUU__OORR() macro adds CPUs present in _s_r_c to _d_s_t.  (It is the ccppuusseett((99))
     equivalent of the scalar: _d_s_t |= _s_r_c.)  CCPPUU__OORR__AATTOOMMIICC() is similar, but
     sets the bits representing CPUs in the component machine words in _d_s_t
     with atomic machine instructions.  (That is, if _d_s_t is composed of multi‐
     ple machine words, CCPPUU__OORR__AATTOOMMIICC() performs multiple individually atomic
     operations.)

     The CCPPUU__OORRNNOOTT() macro add CPUs not in _s_r_c to _d_s_t.  (It is the ccppuusseett((99))
     equivalent of the scalar: _d_s_t |= _~ _s_r_c.)

     The CCPPUU__AANNDD() macro removes CPUs absent from _s_r_c from _d_s_t.  (It is the
     ccppuusseett((99)) equivalent of the scalar: _d_s_t &= _s_r_c.)  CCPPUU__AANNDD__AATTOOMMIICC() is
     similar, with the same atomic semantics as CCPPUU__OORR__AATTOOMMIICC().

     The CCPPUU__AANNDDNNOOTT() macro removes CPUs in _s_r_c from _d_s_t.  (It is the
     ccppuusseett((99)) equivalent of the scalar: _d_s_t &= _~ _s_r_c.)

CCPPUUSSEETT__TT__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR EEXXAAMMPPLLEE
     cpuset_t myset;

     /* Initialize myset to filled (all CPUs) */
     myset = CPUSET_T_INITIALIZER(CPUSET_FSET);

     /* Initialize myset to only the lowest CPU */
     myset = CPUSET_T_INITIALIZER(0x1);

